          "type": "user",
          "text": "Base directory for this skill: /home/leon0399/.claude/skills/gh-stack\n\n# gh-stack\n\n`gh stack` manages a linear chain of branches and pull requests. The trunk is on the left and the\ntop branch is on the right:\n\n```\n(main) <- auth <- api <- frontend\n```\n\nUse this skill when a stack is mentioned, when work has dependent review layers, or when an existing\nstack is being navigated, changed, synchronized, or merged. A single concern normally stays in one\nbranch and PR.\n\n## Invocation boundaries\n\n- Create the stack before writing multi-part code. Foundational work belongs below work that depends\n  on it. Read [references/stack-design.md](references/stack-design.md) when choosing layers.\n- For an existing stack, edit on the branch that owns the change, then rebase branches above it.\n  Inspect `gh stack view --json` and repository history when ownership is unclear.\n- Stacks are strictly linear: one parent and at most one child. Use separate stacks for parallel work.\n- There is no non-interactive reorder or removal. Restructure with `unstack` and `init`; do not use\n  the TUI-only `gh stack modify` from an agent.\n\n## Agent-safe commands\n\n`gh stack` changes behavior under a PTY. Use explicit non-interactive forms:\n\n| Use | Avoid |\n| --- | --- |\n| `gh stack view --json` | bare `gh stack view` |\n| `gh stack submit --auto` | bare `gh stack submit` |\n| `gh stack merge <target> --yes` | `gh pr merge` |\n| `gh stack init <branch>...` | bare `gh stack init` |\n| `gh stack add <branch>` | bare `gh stack add` |\n| `gh stack checkout <target>` | bare `gh stack checkout` |\n| `gh stack up`, `down`, `top`, `bottom` | `gh stack switch` |\n\n`view --short` is safe but human-formatted. `modify` has no non-interactive path.\n\nWith multiple remotes, pass `--remote <name>` to commands that support it or configure\n`remote.pushDefault`; `checkout` and `trunk` rely on that configuration. Check the actual remotes\nbefore changing remote state.\n\nFor first-use setup, read the conditional setup section in\n[references/commands.md](references/commands.md). Do not change repository configuration merely to\ninspect a stack.\n\n## Common loop\n\n```bash\ngh stack init auth\ngit add <auth-files> && git commit -m \"Add auth\"\ngh stack add api\ngit add <api-files> && git commit -m \"Add API\"\ngh stack submit --auto\ngh stack view --json\n```\n\nUse `--open` with `submit` when new PRs should be ready for review. Use `gh stack sync` for the normal\nfetch, reconcile, rebase, push, and PR-state refresh cycle. `--prune` is required for non-interactive\nlocal cleanup of merged branches.\n\n`gh stack merge <target> --yes` merges the target and every unmerged PR below it as one all-or-nothing\nset. A merge queue may override the selected method and land PRs in separate groups. Use\n`gh pr edit` to change titles or bodies generated by `submit --auto`.\n\n## State and recovery\n\n`view --json` writes JSON to stdout and status messages to stderr. Its useful fields are\n`trunk`, `currentBranch`, and `branches[]` (`name`, `head`, `base`, `isCurrent`, `isMerged`,\n`isQueued`, `needsRebase`, and optional PR `number`, `url`, `state`).\n\nRead [references/commands.md](references/commands.md) for command preconditions, side effects,\natomicity, and exit codes. Read [references/troubleshooting.md](references/troubleshooting.md) for\nconflicts, divergence, squash merges, restructuring, shared branches, locks, or interrupted TUI state.\n\n`sync` can exit 0 with `Sync aborted` after detecting local/remote divergence; verify the message or\ninspect JSON before treating it as synchronized. A rebase conflict exits 3; resolve and continue, or\nabort to restore the stack. Push and submit use force-with-lease and are not atomic across branches.\nWhen another tool or worktree drives the branches, read the corresponding `link` guidance in\n`references/troubleshooting.md`.\n",
